<section id="internship-journey" class="py-16 px-6 bg-gray-50">
  <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto">

    <h2 class="text-5xl font-bold mb-12 text-center">
      Internship Journey
    </h2>

    <!-- Clinical Observation -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        Clinical Observation and Rehabilitation Needs Assessment
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        Extensive observation sessions were conducted within Physiotherapy,
        Occupational Therapy, and Augmentative and Alternative Communication
        (AAC) departments. These sessions provided direct exposure to the
        rehabilitation process and the challenges encountered by patients and
        therapists.

        Physiotherapy sessions demonstrated techniques used to improve strength,
        balance, flexibility, coordination, and motor function. Occupational
        Therapy focused on helping individuals perform daily activities
        independently through targeted interventions.

        Several challenges were observed, including pain, fear, reduced
        concentration, delayed understanding, communication difficulties, and
        trust-related barriers. Therapists emphasized maintaining eye contact,
        interacting at the patient’s eye level, and utilizing visual cues and
        picture-based communication systems to improve engagement and establish
        trust.

        The AAC department introduced various communication technologies,
        including communication boards, PECS systems, tactile communication
        aids, speech-generating devices, and applications such as Avaz AAC.
        These observations highlighted the importance of designing assistive
        technologies that are intuitive, accessible, affordable, and
        user-centered.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Literature Survey -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        Problem Analysis and Literature Survey
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        Following the allocation of the project topic, extensive research was
        carried out to understand existing rehabilitation robotics solutions,
        EMG-controlled assistive systems, and current technological
        limitations.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mt-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700">
        <li>EMG signal acquisition techniques</li>
        <li>Signal preprocessing and feature extraction</li>
        <li>Machine learning approaches for intent classification</li>
        <li>Robotic hand exoskeleton designs</li>
        <li>Rehabilitation assessment systems</li>
        <li>Force and angle sensing mechanisms</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        Research papers, conference publications, technical articles, and
        demonstration videos were studied to understand current developments in
        rehabilitation robotics. Discussions with mentors helped refine the
        project scope and identify feasible implementation approaches.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- MSP430 -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        MSP430 Embedded Systems Development
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        The MSP-EXP430FR4133 LaunchPad was introduced as the primary embedded
        development platform for the project. Initial learning focused on GPIO
        operation, LED control, LCD interfacing, analog sensor acquisition,
        memory organization, clock systems, and microcontroller architecture.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6">
        <a
          href="https://www.ti.com/tool/MSP-EXP430FR4133"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
        >
          MSP-EXP430FR4133 Official Documentation →
        </a>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        Initial attempts to program the board were unsuccessful due to limited
        familiarity with the platform. To overcome these challenges,
        datasheets, reference manuals, tutorials, and Texas Instruments
        documentation were studied extensively.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        Several introductory applications were successfully implemented,
        including LED blinking, switch-controlled LEDs, LCD text display, and
        interactive LCD messages. These exercises provided practical experience
        with embedded systems development and real-time hardware interaction.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Debugging -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        Sensor Integration and Hardware Debugging
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        Sensor interfacing experiments were performed using potentiometers,
        ultrasonic sensors, and other peripheral devices. Sensor data was
        successfully acquired and processed using the MSP430 platform.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        Significant debugging efforts were required to resolve LCD display
        issues, undefined register errors, clock configuration problems, LCD
        memory mapping misunderstandings, and JTAG communication failures.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        Through systematic testing and hardware verification, it was discovered
        that the FH-1138P segmented LCD requires two memory bytes per digit
        rather than one. The display driver was redesigned using correct
        segment mapping techniques, resulting in successful character display
        and scrolling text implementation.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        An accelerometer integration attempt was also performed. Although the
        implementation was not successful, it provided valuable experience in
        hardware debugging and communication troubleshooting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Data Logging -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        Data Logging and Monitoring Concepts
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        Data acquisition and storage concepts were explored through the use of
        a microSD card module. Potential applications included recording EMG
        signals, force sensor measurements, rehabilitation session data, and
        long-term patient monitoring information.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        This phase introduced the importance of reliable data storage systems
        for future rehabilitation analytics and clinical assessment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- ESP32 -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        ESP32-S3 Touchscreen Platform Evaluation
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        The Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-4.3 development board was evaluated as
        a potential Human Machine Interface (HMI) platform for the
        rehabilitation device.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-6">
        <a
          href="https://www.waveshare.com/wiki/ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-4.3"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
        >
          ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-4.3 Documentation →
        </a>
      </div>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        Multiple setup challenges were encountered, including COM port
        detection failures, bootloader mode confusion, serial communication
        issues, missing display libraries, and LCD example configuration
        problems.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        These issues were systematically investigated and resolved through
        hardware verification, library installation, board configuration
        adjustments, and communication testing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Dashboard -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        LVGL Rehabilitation Dashboard Development
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        After validating LCD and touchscreen operation, a rehabilitation
        dashboard was developed using LVGL. The dashboard displayed simulated
        rehabilitation parameters such as EMG level, user intent, grip force,
        joint angle, calibration status, and safety alerts.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-8 mt-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700">
        <li>EMG Monitoring</li>
        <li>User Intent Detection</li>
        <li>Grip Force Display</li>
        <li>Joint Angle Visualization</li>
        <li>Calibration Status</li>
        <li>Safety Alerts</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        Interactive controls were implemented to simulate rehabilitation
        workflows. Intent states such as RELAX, ASSIST, and GRIP were added,
        along with force-limit safety mechanisms.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- UI Design -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        User Interface Design and Navigation
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        Several iterations of interface refinement were performed to improve
        readability and usability. Enhancements included larger fonts, improved
        spacing, better widget placement, and navigation controls.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        The final workflow consisted of a Home Screen, Dashboard Screen,
        Stop/Sleep Screen, Wake Screen, and Back Navigation functionality,
        providing a complete user interaction flow.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Calibration -->

    <div class="bg-white border rounded-xl shadow-sm p-10 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-6">
        Calibration System Concept
      </h3>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify">
        A calibration mechanism was conceptualized to address one of the
        project's primary challenges: EMG signal variability caused by
        electrode placement differences and patient-specific physiological
        factors.
      </p>

      <p class="text-gray-700 leading-9 text-justify mt-6">
        The proposed system measures baseline muscle activity before therapy
        begins and automatically adjusts intent-detection thresholds for each
        patient. This personalized approach aims to improve the reliability and
        accuracy of EMG-based intent classification.
      </p>
    </div>

 <!-- Ongoing Project -->

